{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_128","type":"agent_message","text":"# Review round 1 continuation handoff: Joe Biden — United States — 2023\n\n**Job:** `dossier:2023-top20-usa-tha-repair-v1:2023:USA:15811`  \n**Immutable ruler-period:** Joe Biden, United States, 1 January–31 December 2023  \n**Scope:** continuation research for Chapters 1B–8B  \n**Scoring:** none performed  \n**Local-write status:** this worker remains read-only. I could not update `research-materials.md` or `research-ledger-manifest.json`; the additions and manifest delta below are the complete parent-persistence handoff.\n\n## 1. Round-level result\n\nThis continuation materially improved all chapters, with the largest gains in:\n\n- **1B:** South Korean proliferation restraint, nuclear consultation implementation, U.S. posture expansion, and transparency counterevidence.\n- **2B:** direct Biden decisions on cluster munitions, China crisis communications, Gaza hostage-pause diplomacy, UN voting, and Saudi arms transfers.\n- **3B:** DOJ pattern-or-practice enforcement, federal-prison restrictive housing, and UN corroboration of border-policy risks.\n- **4B:** federal social-media contacts, voting-rights enforcement, press treatment, and responses to judicial constraint.\n- **5B:** CHIPS implementation, SAVE implementation, distributional design, and fiscal-policy gaps.\n- **6B:** ACA enrollment, Medicaid unwinding, homelessness, SNAP/rent assistance, and Maui response.\n- **7B:** family-business allegation limits, financial-disclosure practice, cabinet divestiture, and inspector-general omissions.\n- **8B:** concrete program operationalization and implementation milestones, plus documented failures to implement corrective directives.\n\nSeveral source-access limitations persisted:\n\n- Brave tools returned quota-limit errors and furnished no evidence.\n- GAO, CBO, Defense Department, DSCA, and Congress.gov pages frequently blocked fetches.\n- Some official PDFs returned only raw PDF bytes. Where a secondary page extracted precise findings, I retained that secondary claim and preserved the primary document link.\n- Reuters remained robots-blocked and was not promoted from gateway status unless another opened source independently established the fact.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Deterministic cleanup of existing ledger items\n\n## 2.1 Local bundled entries\n\nThe reviewer correctly identified bundled local units. For accounting, split them as follows while preserving the original LF IDs and locators:\n\n- **L001a = LF001** military expenditure, constant-USD field.\n- **L001b = LF002** military expenditure per capita.\n- **L001c = LF003** military expenditure share of GDP.\n- **L001d = LF004** military expenditure share of government expenditure.\n- **L002a–L002f = LF005–LF010**, one item per V-Dem/PTS field.\n- **L003a–L003o = LF011–LF025**, one item per political-freedom field.\n- **L004a–L004c = LF026–LF028**, GDP per capita, total GDP, population.\n- **L005a–L005d = LF029–LF032**, one corruption field per item.\n\nAll remain `context`; none becomes direct ruler evidence.\n\n## 2.2 Split E001 into distinct policy claims\n\nRetire bundled **E001** from strict counting and replace it with:\n\n- **E001a:** reciprocal adherence to New START central limits.\n- **E001b:** offer to engage Russia immediately on a post-2026 framework.\n- **E001c:** offer to engage China without preconditions.\n- **E001d:** proposed P5 missile-launch notification and crisis channels.\n- **E001e:** proposed human-in-the-loop, transparency, cyber/space/AI guardrails.\n\nAll use the same ACA transcript URL but different exact sections and fact keys. They count as five source-claim units but one source organization and one underlying speech.\n\n## 2.3 Split E006\n\nE006 combines distinct Brookings judgments. It should become:\n\n- **E006a:** Biden generally calm and de-escalatory toward major powers.\n- **E006b:** historic Ukraine assistance.\n- **E006c:** repeated 2023 hesitation over weapons decisions.\n- **E006d:** Congress’s material role in aid and security policy.\n\n## 2.4 Split E013 and E014\n\n- **E013a:** official poverty rate fell to 11.1%.\n- **E013b:** SPM rate rose to 12.9%.\n- **E013c:** SPM child poverty rose to 13.7%.\n- **E014a:** poverty/inequality context.\n- **E014b:** incarceration increase and racial disparity.\n- **E014c:** harm-reduction investment.\n- **E014d:** naloxone over-the-counter authorization.\n\nNo field has been discarded; the splits prevent count inflation through bundled provenance.\n\n---\n\n# 3. New evidence-register entries\n\n## Chapter 1B additions\n\n### E021 — Washington Declaration restrained allied proliferation while expanding nuclear consultation\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cfr.org/articles/washington-declaration-expanding-nuclear-dimension-us-south-korean-alliance-response|paragraphs-NCG-NPT|ROK-reaffirms-NPT-in-exchange-for-consultation`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “The Washington Declaration: Expanding the Nuclear Dimension of the U.S.–South Korean Alliance Response”\n- **Publisher/date:** Council on Foreign Relations, 27 April 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** policy institute analysis closely tied to the declaration; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden and Yoon established a Nuclear Consultative Group for joint planning concerning North Korean nuclear use. South Korea reaffirmed its intention to remain in the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty and not pursue an independent arsenal, while the United States retained control over its nuclear weapons.\n- **Precise locator:** Paragraphs beginning “The declaration opens the door…” through “The declaration also reaffirms…”\n- **Excerpt:** “The declaration also reaffirms South Korea’s intention to stay in the Nuclear Nonproliferation Treaty… indirectly pledging not to pursue an independent nuclear weapons capability.”\n- **Period fit:** exact April 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden/Yoon agreement; strong presidential-level attribution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The agreement also expanded nuclear planning and visible deterrence; durability remained unproven.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.4, 1B.6, 1B.7, 1B.8, 1B.10; 2B.3 and 2B.5.\n\n### E022 — Washington Declaration increased visible nuclear deterrence\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.brookings.edu/articles/south-korean-american-pie-unpacking-the-us-south-korea-summit/|section-Ensuring-extended-deterrence|nuclear-submarine-port-visits-and-planning`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “South Korean-American pie: Unpacking the U.S.–South Korea summit”\n- **Publisher/date:** Brookings Institution, 28 April 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** policy analysis; **medium**\n- **Claim:** The declaration gave Seoul a larger consultation role, exchanged this for a nonproliferation commitment, and planned U.S. nuclear-armed submarine port visits to South Korea for the first time in more than 40 years.\n- **Locator:** Section “Ensuring extended deterrence amid North Korean aggression,” paragraphs two and three.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct summit outcome; strong administration and presidential attribution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Nonproliferation reassurance is risk-reducing, while visible nuclear deployments may increase signaling and escalation risk.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.4, 1B.5, 1B.6, 1B.8, 1B.10; 2B.3.\n\n### E023 — Washington Declaration implementation progressed during 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/blogs/new-atlanticist/experts-react/experts-react-are-the-us-and-south-korea-following-through-on-the-washington-declaration/|section-operationalize-institutionalize|tabletop-and-joint-execution`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Experts react: Are the US and South Korea following through on the Washington Declaration?”\n- **Publisher/date:** Atlantic Council, 15 November 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** expert policy analysis citing official defense readouts; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** By November, alliance preparations included the first nuclear-use-focused tabletop exercise, possible inclusion of North Korean nuclear use in a future military exercise, and joint planning for South Korean conventional support to U.S. nuclear operations.\n- **Locator:** “The work has begun to operationalize and institutionalize progress,” second paragraph.\n- **Period fit:** exact November 2023\n- **Attribution:** administration and alliance implementation; Biden-level ownership comes through the April declaration but not each operational detail.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Another expert in the same page said operational plans and institutional distinctions remained unclear.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.3, 1B.6, 1B.7, 1B.8, 1B.10; 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6.\n\n### E024 — Declaration’s novelty and consultation guarantees were limited\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://eastasiaforum.org/2023/07/29/the-washington-declaration-fails-to-address-seouls-nuclear-concerns/|opening-through-Biden-authority-section|declaration-repackaged-prior-measures`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “The Washington Declaration fails to address Seoul’s nuclear concerns”\n- **Publisher/date:** East Asia Forum; 29 July 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** regional academic-policy commentary; **medium**\n- **Claim:** Several declaration components substantially continued arrangements dating from 2017–2022. Biden stated that nuclear-use authority remained solely with the U.S. president and consultation would occur “when it is appropriate,” leaving consultation timing and scope discretionary.\n- **Locator:** Opening seven paragraphs and paragraph beginning “During his press conference…”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden statement and agreement analysis.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The source argues the declaration was less transformative than presented; that does not erase its nonproliferation function.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.3, 1B.6, 1B.8, 1B.10; 8B.1, 8B.6, 8B.10.\n\n### E025 — Biden administration reduced nuclear-stockpile transparency\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://fas.org/publication/while-advocating-nuclear-transparency-abroad-biden-administration-limits-it-at-home/|sections-Contradicting-Previous-Policies-Undercutting-Diplomacy|2023-declassification-denials`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “While Advocating Nuclear Transparency Abroad, Biden Administration Limits It at Home”\n- **Publisher/date:** Federation of American Scientists, 31 July 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist technical NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** DOD and DOE rejected 2023 requests to declassify U.S. stockpile and dismantlement figures. FAS notes Biden restored transparency in 2021 but discontinued it in 2022–2023, forcing diplomats to use older 2020 data while advocating transparency abroad.\n- **Locator:** Sections “Contradicting Previous Policies” and “Undercutting Diplomacy and Democracy.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** executive-branch decision; no evidence that Biden personally directed the denials.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The United States remained more transparent than many nuclear states, and security context following Russia’s invasion may explain—but not independently validate—the reversal.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.3, 1B.4, 1B.7, 1B.10; 4B.6, 4B.10; 7B.2, 7B.6.\n\n### E026 — Primary evidence of possible Lakenheath nuclear-mission restoration\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.fas.org/publication/increasing-evidence-that-the-us-air-forces-nuclear-mission-may-be-returning-to-uk-soil/|paragraphs-budget-documents-formal-decision|potential-surety-mission`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Increasing Evidence That the US Air Force’s Nuclear Mission May Be Returning to UK Soil”\n- **Publisher/date:** Federation of American Scientists, 28 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** technical NGO analyzing Air Force budget documents; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** FY2024 Air Force documents called for a dormitory associated with an arriving “potential Surety mission,” while F-35A and B61-12 infrastructure was being prepared. FAS concluded this strongly suggested preparation for renewed nuclear basing, but emphasized that a formal deployment decision might not yet have been made.\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs beginning “New U.S. Air Force budgetary documents…” and concluding “formal deployment decision has not yet been made.”\n- **Period fit:** exact March–August 2023 planning\n- **Attribution:** Air Force/administration posture, not proven personal Biden direction.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Infrastructure could provide crisis flexibility without permanent deployment or a net increase in European weapons.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.3, 1B.5, 1B.10; contextual 2B.8.\n- **Disposition change:** supersedes E003’s less precise ACA synthesis. Change **E003** from `final_evidence` to `context` to avoid duplicate counting.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 2B additions\n\n### E027 — Biden personally approved cluster-munition transfer to Ukraine\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/07/14/us-cluster-munition-transfer-ukraine-ignores-history-civilian-harm|opening-and-government-rationale|Biden-cluster-munition-decision`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “US Cluster Munition Transfer to Ukraine Ignores History of Civilian Harm”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch, 14 July 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** rights NGO with technical weapons expertise; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden decided to transfer DPICM cluster munitions to Ukraine. U.S. officials justified the transfer as an ammunition bridge, cited lower dud rates, Ukraine’s targeting assurances, and promised clearance support.\n- **Locator:** Opening four paragraphs and section “A Dangerous Substitute.”\n- **Period fit:** exact July 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct personal decision.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Ukraine faced an acute defensive ammunition shortage and promised to record use and avoid cities; HRW argued safeguards could not eliminate civilian and unexploded-ordnance risks.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.3, 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.8, 2B.9, 2B.10.\n\n### E028 — Prior Ukrainian cluster use had caused civilian casualties\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/07/14/us-cluster-munition-transfer-ukraine-ignores-history-civilian-harm|paragraph-Ukraine-Izium|recipient-civilian-harm-record`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** same as E027\n- **Source type/confidence:** NGO; **medium-high**, but contested\n- **Claim:** HRW said it had documented Ukrainian cluster-munition use causing civilian deaths and injuries around Izium; Ukraine denied the findings, and HRW reported no indication of investigation.\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Human Rights Watch has already documented…”\n- **Period fit:** pre-transfer conduct informing a 2023 foreseeability decision\n- **Attribution:** relevant to Biden’s arms-recipient due diligence, not to direct U.S. operational control.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Ukrainian denial must remain visible; this is not a legal judgment.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9.\n\n### E029 — Biden-Xi restored military and leader communications\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-67411191|opening-through-direct-line|military-communications-restored`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “US and China agree to resume military communications”\n- **Publisher/date:** BBC News, 16 November 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** public-service media; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden and Xi agreed to resume military-to-military communications and establish direct leader communication. Biden said absent communication was “how accidents happen.”\n- **Locator:** Opening nine paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact November 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden-Xi decision.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Biden again called Xi a dictator immediately afterward, drawing Chinese condemnation; serious Taiwan disagreements remained.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.8; 2B.1, 2B.3, 2B.7, 2B.10; 8B.8, 8B.9.\n\n### E030 — Biden pressed Netanyahu for a pause tied to hostage release\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.axios.com/2023/11/07/biden-netanayhu-gaza-hamas-ceasefire-pause-hostages|opening-through-behind-scenes|Biden-three-day-pause-proposal`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Scoop: Biden tells Bibi 3-day fighting pause could help secure release of hostages”\n- **Publisher/date:** Axios, 7 November 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable political media citing two U.S./Israeli officials; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden personally urged Netanyahu to accept a three-day pause under a U.S.–Israel–Qatar proposal designed to release an initial 10–15 hostages and verify others.\n- **Locator:** Opening, “The big picture,” and “Behind the scenes.”\n- **Period fit:** exact November 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct presidential diplomacy.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Netanyahu initially resisted; the article precedes the final November pause and alone does not prove Biden caused the eventual agreement.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.1, 2B.4, 2B.7, 2B.10; 8B.3, 8B.9.\n\n### E031 — Biden recognized indiscriminate bombing but U.S. still blocked UN action\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/12/15/us-should-back-un-security-council-action-protect-gazas-civilians|opening-and-voting-record|Biden-warning-versus-US-vetoes`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “US Should Back UN Security Council Action to Protect Gaza’s Civilians”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch, 15 December 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** rights NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden warned Israel it was losing international support because of “indiscriminate bombing,” but the United States had blocked two Security Council resolutions, voted against two General Assembly resolutions, and abstained on one binding child-protection resolution that passed.\n- **Locator:** First five paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact December 2023\n- **Attribution:** Biden-level warning and formal U.S. voting behavior.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The U.S. did permit one binding resolution to pass and consistently demanded hostage release; HRW’s legal framing is advocacy analysis.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.1, 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.7, 2B.9, 2B.10; 7B.8.\n\n### E032 — 2023 Saudi arms-transfer decisions included surveillance and training\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.forumarmstrade.org/us-saudi-arms_biden.html|timeline-December-2023|Saudi-2023-notifications`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden Administration — Arms Sales to Saudi Arabia”\n- **Publisher/date:** Forum on the Arms Trade; continuously maintained, 2023 entries\n- **Source type/confidence:** arms-trade monitoring resource linking DSCA notices; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** In December 2023 the administration notified Congress of a proposed $582 million RE-3A surveillance-aircraft modernization sale and a $1 billion training sale that included civilian-casualty avoidance, law of armed conflict, and human rights.\n- **Locator:** Timeline entries for 4 December and 22 December 2023.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** administration decision; no evidence of personal Biden deliberation.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Proposed sales are not completed deliveries or proof of misuse; the training package included explicit civilian-protection content.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.8, 2B.9; 8B.2.\n\n### E033 — U.S.-supplied weapons had caused Yemen civilian harm before 2023 decisions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/arms-supplied-by-uk-u-s-killed-civilians-in-yemen-report-says|opening-and-Oxfam-findings|Saudi-recipient-harm-baseline`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Arms supplied by UK, U.S. killed civilians in Yemen, report says”\n- **Publisher/date:** PBS NewsHour carrying Associated Press, 11 January 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable public-service media reporting NGO analysis; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Oxfam attributed at least 87 deaths and 136 injuries in 2021–February 2022 Saudi-led airstrikes to U.S./UK-supplied weapons, and reported attacks on health facilities and displacement.\n- **Locator:** Opening and paragraphs presenting Oxfam totals.\n- **Period fit:** pre-2023 recipient-risk baseline, published in 2023\n- **Attribution:** informs foreseeability for continued Biden-era support; does not prove 2023 weapons caused these events.\n- **Contrary/limits:** All Yemen belligerents were accused of civilian harm; Saudi/coalition response was unavailable.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 3B additions\n\n### E034 — Biden DOJ revived systemic police-misconduct enforcement\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/justice-department-reports-reflect-shift-in-thinking-about-police-reform|opening-through-eight-investigations|Biden-DOJ-pattern-practice-revival`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Justice Department Reports Reflect Shift in Thinking About Police Reform”\n- **Publisher/date:** Lawfare, 6 July 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist legal analysis citing DOJ findings; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** DOJ issued its first Biden-era police findings reports in Louisville and Minneapolis and had opened eight police-misconduct investigations in the administration’s first two years, compared with one under Trump.\n- **Locator:** First five paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** executive DOJ enforcement; no evidence that Biden personally managed individual investigations.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Findings identify local abuse, not federal abuse; final remedies and measurable outcomes were still pending.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.7, 3B.8, 3B.10; 4B.3, 4B.4.\n\n### E035 — DOJ findings protected protesters, journalists, and observers\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/justice-department-reports-reflect-shift-in-thinking-about-police-reform|First-Amendment-section|police-attacks-on-journalists-and-observers`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** same as E034\n- **Source type/confidence:** legal analysis; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** DOJ findings identified patterns of First Amendment violations against journalists and people protesting, documenting, or criticizing police, including arrests, attacks, destroyed phones, and a journalist losing an eye to a rubber bullet.\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Finally, both reports found patterns…”\n- **Period fit:** 2023 federal findings concerning earlier/local conduct\n- **Attribution:** positive evidence of federal remedy; adverse incidents are attributable to local departments, not Biden.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.8, 3B.9; 4B.3, 4B.6.\n\n### E036 — Restrictive housing persisted despite Biden directive\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.prisonlegalnews.org/news/2024/aug/15/bop-holding-12000-prisoners-solitary-confinement/|paragraphs-GAO-findings-executive-order|BOP-October-2023-restrictive-housing`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “BOP is Holding 12,000 Prisoners in Solitary Confinement”\n- **Publisher/date:** Prison Legal News, 15 August 2024, summarizing GAO-24-105737\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist prison-rights publication reporting official audit; **medium**\n- **Claim:** As of October 2023, about 8% of federal prisoners—nearly 12,000 people—were in restrictive housing, up to 23 hours per day. Despite Biden’s May 2022 order directing implementation of prior reforms, only 33 of 87 contractor recommendations and 17 of 53 DOJ recommendations had been implemented.\n- **Locator:** First three paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact October 2023 condition, reported retrospectively\n- **Attribution:** federal BOP under Biden; direct personal attribution limited to issuing the corrective order, while implementation failure belongs primarily to DOJ/BOP management.\n- **Contrary/limits:** BOP closed its most restrictive Special Management Unit in 2023.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.10; 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8.\n\n### E037 — UN found January 2023 border measures at variance with refugee protections\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2023/01/new-us-border-enforcement-actions-pose-risk-fundamental-human-rights-turk|entire-release|OHCHR-border-measures-nonrefoulement`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “New US border enforcement actions pose risk to fundamental human rights”\n- **Publisher/date:** Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights, 11 January 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN human-rights mechanism; **high**\n- **Claim:** The High Commissioner said expanded expedited removal and Title 42 appeared at variance with prohibitions on collective expulsion and non-refoulement. He welcomed expanded humanitarian parole but said it could not substitute for individualized asylum assessment.\n- **Locator:** Entire short release, especially paragraphs 1–8.\n- **Period fit:** exact January 2023 policy\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden-administration border measures.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The policy simultaneously expanded a legal humanitarian-parole pathway for up to 30,000 people monthly from four countries.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.9, 3B.10; 4B.7; 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.8.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 4B additions\n\n### E038 — Appeals court found coercion risk in federal social-media contacts\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/court-eases-curbs-biden-administrations-social-media-contacts-rcna104195|opening-through-administration-defense|Fifth-Circuit-social-media-injunction`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Court eases curbs on Biden administration’s social media contacts”\n- **Publisher/date:** NBC News, 8 September 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable media summarizing judicial order; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** The Fifth Circuit barred the White House, surgeon general, CDC, and FBI from coercing or significantly encouraging platforms to remove content, while narrowing a broader district-court injunction. The administration said it requested removal of harmful misinformation but did not compel platforms.\n- **Locator:** First 13 paragraphs and final paragraph.\n- **Period fit:** exact September 2023\n- **Attribution:** executive-branch conduct; evidence does not establish Biden personally directed individual contacts.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The ruling was preliminary, narrowed the lower order, and was later subject to Supreme Court review; it was not a final merits adjudication in 2023.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9, 4B.10; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n\n### E039 — Voting-rights enforcement was restored but restrained\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.democracydocket.com/analysis/the-doj-pledged-to-relentlessly-protect-voting-rights-is-it-living-up-to-its-promise/|section-eight-lawsuits|DOJ-voting-enforcement-scale`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “The DOJ Pledged to Relentlessly Protect Voting Rights. Is It Living Up to Its Promise?”\n- **Publisher/date:** Democracy Docket, 4 May 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** voting-rights legal media/advocacy; **medium**\n- **Claim:** Since 2021 DOJ had filed eight voting-rights/redistricting suits, five active, plus 18 statements of interest. The source found this a clear improvement over Trump but only 2.4% of the 210 active cases it tracked; former Biden chief of staff Ron Klain called DOJ too cautious.\n- **Locator:** Section “Since 2021, the DOJ has filed eight…” and section on amicus/statements of interest.\n- **Period fit:** exact May 2023\n- **Attribution:** DOJ enforcement under Biden; institutional independence and Attorney General discretion limit