**Publisher/date:** archived White House, 22 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high** for formal action, medium-low for self-assessed effects\n- **Claim:** After the Supreme Court rejected the broad cancellation plan, the administration announced it was pursuing a different legal route and launched an income-driven repayment regulation rather than implementing the invalidated program.\n- **Locator:** “Broader Efforts to Deliver Relief,” final paragraph.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden-administration response.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The official source frames the action favorably; whether the new route fully respected statutory limits was separately litigated later.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.4, 4B.8, 4B.10; 5B.7, 5B.9; 8B.8.\n\n### E042 — Press-freedom safeguards and unresolved 2023 prosecution concerns\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://freedom.press/issues/bidens-press-freedom-legacy-empty-words-and-hypocrisy/|sections-anti-press-prosecutions-and-positive-actions|Biden-press-freedom-mixed`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Biden’s press freedom legacy: Empty words and hypocrisy”\n- **Publisher/date:** Freedom of the Press Foundation, January 2025\n- **Source type/confidence:** press-freedom advocacy; **medium**\n- **Claim:** The retrospective credits Biden DOJ guidelines limiting compulsory process and surveillance of reporters and warnings against arresting journalists at protests, but criticizes continuation of the Assange prosecution and later use of computer-crime theories against journalist Tim Burke.\n- **Locator:** Sections “Anti-press prosecutions” and final paragraphs beginning “Yes, Biden’s done some good things…”\n- **Period fit:** retrospective; only underlying 2023 acts should be used\n- **Attribution:** DOJ/administration policy; direct Biden role varies.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Highly critical advocacy retrospective and includes post-2023 events; not valid for silently extending the target period.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.10; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5B additions\n\n### E043 — CHIPS funding mechanism launched with milestones and safeguards\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2023/02/biden-harris-administration-launches-first-chips-america-funding|opening-and-program-priorities|first-CHIPS-funding-opportunity`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden-Harris Administration Launches First CHIPS for America Funding Opportunity”\n- **Publisher/date:** NIST, 28 February 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high** for implementation facts\n- **Claim:** Commerce launched the first manufacturing-incentive opportunity under a $50 billion program. Awards were to be disbursed against milestones, require due diligence, encourage private co-investment, and evaluate commercial viability, technical readiness, workforce plans, childcare, and inclusive growth.\n- **Locator:** Opening five paragraphs and bullet sections “Protecting Taxpayer Dollars,” “Building a Skilled and Diverse Workforce,” and “Driving Economic Opportunity.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** legislation was bipartisan; administration owned program design and execution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Launch and design are not completed production or proven economic effects.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.2, 5B.4, 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.8; 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.4, 8B.6.\n\n### E044 — First CHIPS incentive and private-investment claims by end of 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.semiconductors.org/commerce-department-announcement-marks-milestone-in-chips-implementation/|opening-and-investment-paragraph|first-CHIPS-incentive`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Commerce Department Announcement Marks Milestone in CHIPS Implementation”\n- **Publisher/date:** Semiconductor Industry Association, 11 December 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** industry association; **medium**\n- **Claim:** Commerce announced the first semiconductor manufacturing incentive in December 2023. SIA said industry projects announced since CHIPS was introduced totaled over $200 billion and were expected to create more than 40,000 direct ecosystem jobs.\n- **Locator:** Opening statement and paragraph beginning “The CHIPS Act’s manufacturing incentives…”\n- **Period fit:** exact December 2023\n- **Attribution:** administration implementation plus private-sector announcements; causal attribution to CHIPS and Biden is partial.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Industry has strong incentives to praise subsidies; announced investments and projected jobs are not completed outcomes.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.4, 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.10; 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.10.\n\n### E045 — SAVE plan converted policy into concrete repayment rules\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/08/22/fact-sheet-the-biden-harris-administration-launches-the-save-plan-the-most-affordable-student-loan-repayment-plan-ever-to-lower-monthly-payments-for-millions-of-borrowers/|specific-SAVE-plan-rules|SAVE-operational-design`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** archived White House fact sheet, 22 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high** for program rules\n- **Claim:** SAVE based payments on income and household size, raised protected income to 225% of poverty, prevented uncovered monthly interest from increasing balances, and established earlier forgiveness for low original balances.\n- **Locator:** Bullets “Cut payments,” “Bring many borrowers’ payments to $0,” “Ensure…balance,” and “Provide early forgiveness.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 launch\n- **Attribution:** direct Biden program, implemented by Education Department regulation.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Administration estimates of savings and beneficiary counts are self-reported; later litigation is outside the target period.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.9; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.8; 8B.2, 8B.6.\n- **Disposition change:** promote **E015** from `discovery_only` to `rejected`, reason: `Superseded by opened archived White House primary record E045; Reuters gateway no longer needed`.\n\n### E046 — SAVE distributional design targeted lower-income and minority borrowers\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/08/22/fact-sheet-the-biden-harris-administration-launches-the-save-plan-the-most-affordable-student-loan-repayment-plan-ever-to-lower-monthly-payments-for-millions-of-borrowers/|distributional-estimates|SAVE-progressive-payment-incidence`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title/publisher/date:** same as E045\n- **Source type/confidence:** official; **medium-low** for projected effects\n- **Claim:** Education Department projections said payments per dollar borrowed would decline most for the lowest projected earners and that Black, Hispanic, American Indian, and Alaska Native borrowers would experience larger reductions.\n- **Locator:** Bullet list under “The benefits…particularly critical.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 projection\n- **Attribution:** program design attributable to administration.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Projections, not observed 2023 outcomes; require independent evaluation.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.8; 6B.3 and 6B.8.\n\n### E047 — Fiscal deficit remained large; direct Biden attribution unresolved\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58946|search-index-summary|CBO-2023-deficit-projection`\n- **Disposition:** discovery_only\n- **Title:** “The Budget and Economic Outlook: 2023 to 2033”\n- **Publisher/date:** Congressional Budget Office, 15 February 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official independent budget analysis; potentially **high**\n- **Claim candidate:** CBO projected a 2023 federal deficit around $1.4 trillion, approximately 5.3% of GDP.\n- **Locator status:** `underlying_source_missing`; CBO blocked fetch, and search-result text is not a sufficient locator.\n- **Mappings if repaired:** 5B.3, 5B.9, 5B.10; 8B.7.\n- **Manifest note:** retain as discovery only, not evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 6B additions\n\n### E048 — Record 2023 Marketplace plan selections\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/marketplace-2023-open-enrollment-period-report-final-national-snapshot|opening-and-national-table|2023-marketplace-enrollment`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Marketplace 2023 Open Enrollment Period Report: Final National Snapshot”\n- **Publisher/date:** Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official administrative data; **high**\n- **Claim:** About 16.3 million people selected 2023 Marketplace plans, including 3.6 million new consumers and 12.7 million returning consumers.\n- **Locator:** Opening paragraph and first national table.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 coverage selection\n- **Attribution:** federal subsidies/outreach and state marketplaces contributed; Biden cannot receive sole causal attribution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Plan selection is not necessarily effectuated enrollment; the report says first premiums may still need payment.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.10; 5B.8.\n\n### E049 — 2024 enrollment surge partly absorbed Medicaid losses occurring in 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/marketplace-2024-open-enrollment-period-report-final-national-snapshot|opening-through-Medicaid-transition|2023-year-end-marketplace-transition`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Marketplace 2024 Open Enrollment Period Report: Final National Snapshot”\n- **Publisher/date:** CMS, early 2024, covering enrollment beginning November 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official administrative data; **high**\n- **Claim:** 21.3 million selected 2024 plans; by 31 December 2023, 2.4 million HealthCare.gov selections came from people previously enrolled in Medicaid or CHIP.\n- **Locator:** First two paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** directly captures November–December 2023 transition activity, with final totals extending into January 2024\n- **Attribution:** administration Marketplace operation; Medicaid unwinding was legislatively mandated and state-administered.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Does not prove continuous coverage or absence of gaps.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.10; 8B.8.\n\n### E050 — Medicaid unwinding created large, uneven coverage-loss risk\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.kff.org/medicaid/10-things-to-know-about-the-unwinding-of-the-medicaid-continuous-enrollment-provision/|intro-and-sections-1-3|Medicaid-unwinding-risk`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “10 Things to Know About the Unwinding of the Medicaid Continuous Enrollment Provision”\n- **Publisher/date:** KFF, 9 June 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** health-policy research organization; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** The CAA ended continuous enrollment on 31 March 2023. KFF projected 7.8–24.4 million losses, while HHS projected up to 15 million disenrollments, including 6.8 million people likely still eligible; state processes and outreach would determine much of the harm.\n- **Locator:** Introduction and sections 1–3.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 policy transition\n- **Attribution:** Congress enacted the end date; Biden signed the law and CMS supervised, while states administered renewals. Attribution must remain shared.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Not everyone disenrolled would become uninsured, and some would shift to Marketplace or employer coverage.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.10; 5B.8; 8B.5, 8B.9.\n\n### E051 — Later data show procedural failures dominated many disenrollments\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.kff.org/medicaid/medicaid-enrollment-tracker/|archived-unwinding-summary|procedural-disenrollments`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Medicaid/CHIP Monthly Enrollment Tracker”\n- **Publisher/date:** KFF, archived unwinding data last updated 12 September 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** health-policy data synthesis using CMS/state reports; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Across the full unwinding, 25.2 million people were disenrolled; 69% of disenrollments were procedural rather than findings of ineligibility. State rates varied from 12% to 57%.\n- **Locator:** “Unwinding Data – Archived,” summary bullets.\n- **Period fit:** retrospective and extends well beyond 2023; useful only to evaluate implementation of a process started in 2023\n- **Attribution:** state and federal systems jointly responsible; cannot be placed solely on Biden.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.9; 8B.5, 8B.8.\n\n### E052 — Homelessness rose 12% in the 2023 point-in-time count\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://nlihc.org/resource/hud-releases-2023-annual-homeless-assessment-report|opening-and-population-sections|2023-homelessness-increase`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “HUD Releases 2023 Annual Homeless Assessment Report”\n- **Publisher/date:** National Low Income Housing Coalition, 18 December 2023, summarizing HUD AHAR\n- **Source type/confidence:** housing-policy NGO reporting official HUD data; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Approximately 653,100 people experienced homelessness on one night in 2023, a 12% increase and the highest reported count since 2007. Family homelessness rose 16%, and racial/ethnic minorities remained heavily overrepresented.\n- **Locator:** Opening four paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 count\n- **Attribution:** national outcome; housing supply, rent, pandemic-support expiration, state/local policy, migration, and economy complicate Biden attribution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Point-in-time count is a snapshot. The source says American Rescue Plan resources had prevented increases in 2020–2022 but largely expired.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.8, 5B.10; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.10.\n\n### E053 — SNAP and emergency rental assistance improved material security\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2024/24_0121.htm|abstract-results-and-introduction|SNAP-rent-assistance-material-security`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “SNAP Emergency Allotments, Emergency Rent Assistance, Rent Burden, and Housing and Food Security, June 2022–May 2023”\n- **Publisher/date:** CDC Preventing Chronic Disease, 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** peer-reviewed government journal; **high**\n- **Claim:** Using Household Pulse Survey data, the study found SNAP emergency allotments and emergency rental assistance were associated with greater food sufficiency; rental aid was also associated with being current on rent.\n- **Locator:** Abstract “Results” and opening introduction.\n- **Period fit:** June 2022–May 2023, partly target year\n- **Attribution:** programs originated through federal legislation and involved state/local administration; not solely Biden-created.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Observational association, not randomized causation; benefits differed by rent burden.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.8; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.10.\n\n### E054 — Ending SNAP emergency allotments reduced benefits and raised insufficiency\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2024/24_0121.htm|introduction-emergency-allotment-expiration|SNAP-ending-harm`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** same as E053\n- **Source type/confidence:** peer-reviewed government journal; **high**\n- **Claim:** The study reports prior evidence that ending emergency allotments reduced benefits by an estimated $90 per person monthly and increased recent food insufficiency among SNAP households from 25% to 31%.\n- **Locator:** Introduction paragraph beginning “Food security…,” through the emergency-allotment discussion.\n- **Period fit:** policy ended nationally in February 2023\n- **Attribution:** Congress established the expiration through the 2023 appropriations law signed by Biden; shared attribution.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Study cites prior analysis for the causal estimate.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.10; 5B.8.\n\n### E055 — Rapid federal mobilization after Maui fires\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-biden-harris-administrations-response-the-maui-wildfires|opening-through-response-bullets|Maui-federal-mobilization`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Fact Sheet: Biden-Harris Administration’s Response to the Maui Wildfires”\n- **Publisher/date:** American Presidency Project reproducing White House document, 15 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high** for enumerated deployments\n- **Claim:** Biden signed a major-disaster declaration within hours; by 15 August nearly 500 federal personnel were deployed, including over 140 urban-search-and-rescue personnel, and FEMA reported deliveries of meals, water, cots, blankets, and $700 critical-needs assistance.\n- **Locator:** Opening three paragraphs and response bullet list.\n- **Period fit:** exact August 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct disaster declaration and federal mobilization.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Official self-report establishes resources, not adequacy, equitable access, or long-term effectiveness.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.1, 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.4, 6B.6, 6B.10; 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.9.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 7B additions\n\n### E056 — 2023 family-business inquiry had not established Joe Biden’s involvement\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-republicans-hold-first-hearing-biden-impeachment-inquiry-rcna117657|opening-through-witness-evidence|impeachment-hearing-no-president-crime-evidence`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “House GOP’s impeachment witnesses say no evidence of crime by President Biden”\n- **Publisher/date:** NBC News, 28 September 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable media reporting congressional hearing; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Republican-selected expert witnesses said the existing record did not establish a crime by Joe Biden. One witness said evidence was missing on whether Biden knew of or encouraged influence peddling; bank records presented at the hearing showed no payment to the president, according to the committee’s ranking Democrat.\n- **Locator:** Opening 15 paragraphs and paragraph discussing Turley’s missing evidence.\n- **Period fit:** exact September 2023\n- **Attribution:** directly concerns allegations against Biden.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The witnesses said further inquiry was warranted; absence of evidence at that hearing is not proof no relevant conduct occurred.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.3, 7B.4, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10.\n\n### E057 — Financial disclosures were released and OGE-certified\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/white-house-press-release-the-president-and-vice-president-release-their-financial-1|entire-release|Biden-2023-financial-disclosure`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “The President and Vice President Release Their Financial Disclosure Reports”\n- **Publisher/date:** American Presidency Project reproducing White House release, 15 May 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high** for filing and certification\n- **Claim:** Biden released his annual public OGE Form 278, which was reviewed and certified by the Office of Government Ethics.\n- **Locator:** Entire short release.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct personal disclosure.\n- **Contrary/limits:** The release’s statement that no conflicts existed is self-serving and cannot substitute for independent review; the underlying PDF could not be opened in this run.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.3, 7B.4, 7B.6, 7B.10.\n\n### E058 — Senior officials largely complied with divestiture obligations\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.citizensforethics.org/reports-investigations/crew-reports/biden-administration-financial-disclosures-show-strong-pattern-of-compliance/|opening-and-divestiture-section|cabinet-ethics-compliance`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden administration financial disclosures show strong pattern of compliance”\n- **Publisher/date:** Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, 17 April 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** nonpartisan ethics NGO analysis of OGE filings; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** CREW found a high degree of compliance by senior Biden officials with divestiture obligations; cabinet members generally divested individual stocks and conflicting assets, and at least one went beyond required divestitures.\n- **Locator:** Opening paragraph and “Biden cabinet members complied…”/“Individual stock holdings” sections.\n- **Period fit:** 2023 review of 2021–2022 filings; administration-pattern context\n- **Attribution:** appointments and ethics framework attributable to administration; not proof of Biden’s own finances or every appointee’s competence.\n- **Contrary/limits:** CREW still recommended stronger law barring senior officials from individual stocks and family-business interests.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.3, 7B.5, 7B.6, 7B.9, 7B.10; 8B.4.\n\n### E059 — Biden failed to act on documented inspector-general concerns\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.pogo.org/policy-letters/president-biden-remove-inspectors-general-cuffari-and-ennis-immediately|opening-conclusion-and-mission-failures|Biden-IG-inaction`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “President Biden: Remove Inspectors General Cuffari and Ennis Immediately”\n- **Publisher/date:** Project On Government Oversight, 21 September 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** watchdog NGO with cited investigative record; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** POGO urged Biden to remove DHS IG Joseph Cuffari and SSA IG Gail Ennis, citing suppression of findings, deleted federal texts, whistleblower-retaliation claims, weak death-in-custody review, and staff dysfunction. POGO explicitly attributed ongoing harm to Biden’s lack of action.\n- **Locator:** Opening, “Failing in Their Missions,” “Endangering the Public,” and conclusion.\n- **Period fit:** exact September 2023\n- **Attribution:** Biden had removal/nomination authority, but removal of inspectors general presents independence concerns and requires due process/context.\n- **Contrary/limits:** This is an advocacy demand, not an adjudicated finding against Biden; evidence concerns the IGs’ conduct and Biden’s inaction, not Biden-directed retaliation.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.2, 7B.5, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10; 8B.4, 8B.5, 8B.8.\n\n### E060 — Personnel favoritism lead could not be upgraded\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://campaignlegal.org/sites/default/files/2023-05/DTA_Report_Final.pdf|pdf|donor-ambassador-report`\n- **Disposition:** rejected\n- **Reason:** PDF returned raw bytes and no reliable page/paragraph locator. Search snippets indicated a donor-to-ambassador analysis, but it cannot be used as evidence without extraction.\n- **Potential mappings:** 7B.5, 7B.9; 8B.4.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 8B additions\n\n### E061 — CHIPS program established explicit goals, evaluation, personnel, and controls\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2023/02/biden-harris-administration-launches-first-chips-america-funding|Vision-for-Success-and-program-offices|CHIPS-goals-and-administration`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** NIST CHIPS funding launch, 28 February 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official primary record; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** The administration established measurable end-decade fabrication/packaging goals, separate CHIPS Program and R&D offices, staged funding opportunities, formal application criteria, and milestone-linked disbursement.\n- **Locator:** “Vision for Success,” funding-process paragraphs, and final paragraph identifying program offices.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** executive implementation of bipartisan statute.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Architecture and plans alone do not demonstrate outcomes.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.4, 8B.5, 8B.6.\n\n### E062 — First CHIPS award arrived late in the target year\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.semiconductors.org/commerce-department-announcement-marks-milestone-in-chips-implementation/|first-paragraph|first-incentive-December-2023`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** SIA, 11 December 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** industry association; **medium**\n- **Claim:** The first manufacturing incentive was announced in December, showing movement from program design to award but also that most direct awards remained outstanding through most of 2023.\n- **Locator:** Opening statement.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** Commerce implementation; Congress supplied statutory authority and funds.\n- **Contrary/limits:** SIA’s praise is interested; the first award cannot establish portfolio-wide success.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.10.\n\n### E063 — SAVE showed rapid adaptation after judicial defeat\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/08/22/fact-sheet-the-biden-harris-administration-launches-the-save-plan-the-most-affordable-student-loan-repayment-plan-ever-to-lower-monthly-payments-for-millions-of-borrowers/|launch-outreach-and-automation|SAVE-adaptation-and-delivery`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** archived White House, 22 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official; **medium-high** for formal design\n- **Claim:** Within two months of the Supreme Court ruling, the administration launched SAVE, planned outreach to nearly 30 million borrowers, automatically moved REPAYE borrowers, streamlined applications, and designed automatic recertification and delinquent-borrower enrollment.\n- **Locator:** Opening and “Department of Education Launches Outreach Campaign.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct administration adaptation.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Official launch does not prove uptake or legal durability; later litigation falls outside period.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n### E064 — Prison reform showed weak follow-through despite clear direction\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.prisonlegalnews.org/news/2024/aug/15/bop-holding-12000-prisoners-solitary-confinement/|executive-order-and-GAO-implementation|BOP-corrective-failure`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** Prison Legal News, 15 August 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist publication citing GAO; **medium**\n- **Claim:** Biden’s executive order provided clear direction to complete restrictive-housing reforms, but by October 2023 BOP had made slow progress and lacked responsibility, verification, and data-analysis systems.\n- **Locator:** Second through fourth paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact October 2023 status\n- **Attribution:** Biden supplied strategic direction; DOJ/BOP carried implementation responsibility.\n- **Contrary/limits:** Closure of the Special Management Unit was a concrete corrective step.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.2, 8B.4, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n### E065 — Border-policy adaptation combined legal pathways with coercive enforcement\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2023/01/new-us-border-enforcement-actions-pose-risk-fundamental-human-rights-turk|policy-description|parole-plus-expedited-removal`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title/publisher/date:** OHCHR, 11 January 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN; **high**\n- **Claim:** The administration combined expanded parole for up to 30,000 people monthly from four countries with expanded expedited removals and Title 42 expulsions.\n- **Locator:** Policy-description paragraphs.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct administration program.\n- **Morality separation:** For 8B, this item establishes operational policy adaptation and mixed instruments; human-rights merits belong in 3B/6B.\n- **Contrary/limits:** No outcome evidence here showing durable border control or humane processing.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.6, 8B.9.\n\n### E066 — Maui response mobilized multiple agencies rapidly\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-biden-harris-administrations-response-the-maui-wildfires|whole-of-government-and-agency-list|Maui-coordination`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title/publisher/date:** American Presidency Project/White House, 15 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** official; **medium-high** for deployments\n- **Claim:** FEMA, Coast Guard, Navy, Army, HHS, Forest Service, SBA, EPA, Agriculture, and Army Corps were mobilized under a federal response with personnel, supplies, public-health measures, and command support.\n- **Locator:** Opening and bullet list.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct presidential declaration, implementation distributed across agencies and state/local partners.\n- **Contrary/limits:** No independent evaluation of adequacy or long-term recovery in the opened target-period sources.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.9.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Updated chapter yields and saturation findings\n\nCounts below are approximate after deterministic splitting. Reuse is many-to-many; global unique-item counts are lower than chapter-mapped counts.\n\n## 1B\n\n- **New defensible units:** E021–E026, plus E001a–E001e and E002.\n- **Independent organizations:** ACA/White House, CFR, Brookings, Atlantic Council, East Asia Forum, FAS.\n- **Improved gaps:** direct Biden/Yoon nonproliferation choice; presidential launch-authority statement; 2023 operationalization; posture expansion; transparency.\n- **Still unresolved:** detailed NC3 safety, actual stockpile start/end numbers, Iran/Saudi nonproliferation negotiations, biological-risk implementation, and a definitive whole-posture comparison.\n- **Saturation:** substantially improved and above minimum yield, but not fully saturated because command-and-control and end-posture data remain technically inaccessible/classified.\n\n## 2B\n\n- **New defensible units:** E027–E033, plus E029/E030/E031.\n- **Independent organizations:** HRW, BBC, Axios, UN, CFR/Brookings, Forum on Arms Trade, AP/PBS.\n- **Improved gaps:** cluster-munition decision and recipient risk; China crisis channels; Biden’s hostage-pause proposal; Gaza UN voting and warning; Saudi notifications.\n- **Still unresolved:** ATACMS and F-16 direct decision records, Israel emergency-sale primary DSCA records, independent proof that Biden caused the eventual November pause, and U.S. response to specific civilian-harm incidents.\n- **Saturation:** strong thematic coverage, but arms-recipient accountability remains incomplete because official transfer pages were blocked.\n\n## 3B\n\n- **New defensible units:** E034–E037 plus E036.\n- **Independent organizations:** DOJ-derived Lawfare, UN/OHCHR, GAO-derived prison reporting, ACLU, HRW.\n- **Improved gaps:** pattern/practice enforcement, protest/journalist protection, prison isolation, independent border corroboration.\n- **Still unresolved:** 2023 federal protest surveillance and directly extracted DHS OIG death reports. Raw PDFs were not usable.\n- **Saturation:** adequate for principal federal-action themes; not saturated for prison deaths and surveillance.\n\n## 4B\n\n- **New units:** E038–E042, E025.\n- **Independent organizations:** NBC/judicial record, Democracy Docket, CPJ, Freedom of the Press Foundation, FAS, official White House.\n- **Improved gaps:** CISA/social-media contacts; voting enforcement; press access; transparency; response to court loss.\n- **Still unresolved:** personality cult/loyalty tests remain largely unsupported—targeted searches did not reveal a material 2023 pattern. Transfer-of-power behavior is not a discriminating 2023 event.\n- **Saturation:** reasonable for federal constraints and media; weak lenses are now documented as searched rather than silently inferred.\n\n## 5B\n\n- **New units:** E043–E47, with E045 replacing E015.\n- **Independent organizations:** NIST, SIA, official White House, CMS/KFF for distributional spillovers.\n- **Improved gaps:** program design, milestones, distributional targeting, court-driven correction.\n- **Still unresolved:** CBO deficit and independent inflation attribution remain discovery-only because CBO blocked access. IRA and infrastructure implementation audits were also inaccessible.\n- **Saturation:** policy execution improved from thin to moderate; fiscal/macro causal analysis remains materially incomplete.\n\n## 6B\n\n- **New units:** E048–E055.\n- **Independent organizations:** CMS, KFF, NLIHC/HUD, CDC, White House/APP.\n- **Improved gaps:** ACA enrollment, Medicaid unwinding, housing/homelessness, SNAP, rent assistance, disaster response.\n- **Still unresolved:** maternal health, reproductive care, rural access, school recovery, childcare, and independent Maui adequacy evaluation.\n- **Saturation:** now diversified and above minimum; still not fully saturated across all service domains.\n\n## 7B\n\n- **New units:** E056–E060, plus E025/E038/E042 as cross-chapter items.\n- **Independent organizations:** NBC, APP/OGE, CREW, POGO, FAS, press-freedom groups.\n- **Improved gaps:** family-business evidentiary boundary; disclosures; cabinet divestiture; watchdog response.\n- **Still unresolved:** underlying Biden Form 278 details, donor-ambassador report extraction, campaign-finance patterns, and a broad correction/candor sample.\n- **Saturation:** no longer rests only on classified documents, but personal-integrity attribution remains uneven. No unsupported family-corruption allegation was promoted.\n\n## 8B\n\n- **New units:** E061–E066 plus implementation reuse from other chapters.\n- **Independent organizations:** NIST, SIA, White House, GAO-derived prison report, UN, Atlantic Council, CMS.\n- **Improved gaps:** strategy-to-program translation, milestone-linked administration, adaptation, interagency crisis response, and implementation failure.\n- **Still unresolved:** full portfolio scorecard, cabinet turnover and personnel performance, IRA/infrastructure audit findings, and independent budget execution.\n- **Saturation:** strong for selected programs, still incomplete for the frozen portfolio as a whole.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Strongest new contrary evidence\n\n1. **Nuclear policy:** Washington Declaration restrained South Korean proliferation, but also expanded nuclear consultation and strategic-asset visibility; U.S. transparency declined and Lakenheath preparations suggested posture expansion.\n2. **Peace/security:** Biden restored China military communications and pressed for a Gaza pause, but approved cluster munitions and maintained arms relationships despite documented recipient civilian-harm risks.\n3. **Domestic safety:** DOJ revived systemic police oversight, but federal restrictive-housing reform remained largely unimplemented and immigration policies raised UN non-refoulement concerns.\n4. **Political freedom:** DOJ pursued voting rights and press safeguards, while courts found a plausible coercion problem in social-media contacts and press-access concerns persisted.\n5. **Economy:** CHIPS and SAVE moved from plans into administrative implementation, but end-2023 production/outcome effects were limited or projected, and fiscal/macro attribution remained unresolved.\n6. **Social welfare:** Marketplace enrollment expanded and emergency supports demonstrably improved security, but Medicaid procedural losses, homelessness, child poverty, and food-support expiration were significant.\n7. **Integrity:** Independent inquiry evidence had not tied Joe Biden to family-business crimes, and disclosures/divestiture compliance were positive; classifi